{"slug": "induction-motor-slip-explained", "targets": ["slip percentage formula"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">Slip Percentage Formula Quick Reference</h2><p>The <strong>slip percentage formula</strong> is %S = (Ns − Nr) ÷ Ns × 100, where Ns is synchronous speed and Nr is rotor speed. Rearranged, rotor speed is Nr = Ns × (1 − S), and the frequency of current induced in the rotor is fr = S × f. These three forms let you find slip, speed or rotor frequency from whichever two values you know.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Poles (50 Hz)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Ns (rpm)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Example Nr (rpm)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Slip %</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Rotor frequency (Hz)</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">3000</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2940</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1500</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1470</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">6</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1000</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">960</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">8</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">750</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">720</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2</td></tr></table></div><p>Steps to work out slip percentage on a running motor:</p><ol><li>Read supply frequency and pole count from the nameplate.</li><li>Calculate Ns = 120 × f ÷ P.</li><li>Measure shaft speed Nr with a tachometer.</li><li>Apply %S = (Ns − Nr) ÷ Ns × 100.</li></ol><p>At standstill Nr is zero, so slip is 100 percent and rotor frequency equals supply frequency. As load rises, rotor speed falls a little and slip percentage increases, which raises rotor current and torque.</p>"}